1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,886
After one edit to the skill, the passing
trials on one model went from five of nine

2
00:00:04,886 --> 00:00:07,750
to nine of nine, and the two ranges still
overlapped.

3
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:11,850
So those trials couldn't yet say the edit
made things better.

4
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:16,732
Before every change we keep, there's a
narrower question: is it any worse than

5
00:00:16,732 --> 00:00:17,125
before?

6
00:00:17,625 --> 00:00:22,078
Here, you'll judge two saved runs side by
side, and run a check that's built to fail

7
00:00:22,078 --> 00:00:23,775
when a change made things worse.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,875
Let's start with what our graders can't
see.

9
00:00:28,375 --> 00:00:32,775
Each grader takes one trial at a time, and
holds it against a fixed check.

10
00:00:33,275 --> 00:00:37,620
That can't answer a question like: is the
new skill's entry better than the old

11
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:37,950
one's?

12
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:42,557
Here are two entries for the same request,
and the graders passed both, so the

13
00:00:42,557 --> 00:00:44,400
difference between them disappears.

14
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:49,650
A one-to-five scale has the same limit,
since it places each entry by itself.

15
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:53,467
A pairwise judge works differently.

16
00:00:53,967 --> 00:00:58,148
It's an LLM judge that's shown two answers
to the same request, one from each

17
00:00:58,148 --> 00:01:00,917
version, and says which is better, and by
how much.

18
00:01:01,417 --> 00:01:06,208
Comparing two answers side by side is
easier than scoring either one alone, for

19
00:01:06,208 --> 00:01:07,542
people and for models.

20
00:01:08,042 --> 00:01:11,642
That's why a pairwise judge notices
smaller changes.

21
00:01:12,867 --> 00:01:15,292
It has one well-known weakness.

22
00:01:15,792 --> 00:01:20,952
Shown two answers, a judge tends to prefer
one position, often the first, whatever is

23
00:01:20,952 --> 00:01:21,317
in it.

24
00:01:21,817 --> 00:01:25,767
So Vally asks twice, with the two answers
in the opposite order.

25
00:01:26,267 --> 00:01:29,617
When both orders agree, the pair gets that
verdict.

26
00:01:30,117 --> 00:01:33,167
When they disagree, the pair is scored as
a tie.

27
00:01:34,367 --> 00:01:37,392
A pairwise judge doesn't replace graders.

28
00:01:37,892 --> 00:01:41,967
It can't say that both versions are wrong,
only which one is less wrong.

29
00:01:42,467 --> 00:01:45,517
So graders stay for the question: is it
right?

30
00:01:46,017 --> 00:01:49,942
The side-by-side judgement is for the
other one: is it worse?

31
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:54,067
That's the idea; here's the command that
does it.

32
00:01:54,567 --> 00:01:59,250
Vally compare runs a pairwise judge over
two sets of saved trials, and adds up the

33
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:01,192
result, without starting an agent.

34
00:02:01,692 --> 00:02:06,173
Its baseline is the version we compare
against, and Vally's word for the version

35
00:02:06,173 --> 00:02:07,742
we changed is the treatment.

36
00:02:08,242 --> 00:02:12,110
Here, both are variants of our
before-and-after experiment: one model,

37
00:02:12,110 --> 00:02:14,542
with the skill before the edit and after
it.

38
00:02:15,042 --> 00:02:19,333
Next, the judge has to be named with dash
dash judge model, because this command

39
00:02:19,333 --> 00:02:23,517
doesn't take it from the eval file, and
its default may be a model under test.

40
00:02:24,017 --> 00:02:28,192
Finally, dash dash output names a file for
a record of every pair.

41
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:33,275
Vally compare can also be given a whole
experiment folder.

42
00:02:33,775 --> 00:02:37,910
Then the baseline is the one the
experiment file names, here haiku before,

43
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:40,425
and every other variant is judged against
it.

44
00:02:40,925 --> 00:02:45,399
That would set the terra variants against
haiku, and mix a change of model into a

45
00:02:45,399 --> 00:02:46,725
question about the edit.

46
00:02:47,225 --> 00:02:51,175
So we'll name two variant folders instead,
one model at a time.

47
00:02:51,675 --> 00:02:56,175
Each holds its own results dot jsonl,
which is all compare needs.

48
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:02,493
Vally pairs the trials by stimulus name
and trial number: trial zero with trial

49
00:03:02,493 --> 00:03:03,525
zero, and so on.

50
00:03:04,025 --> 00:03:08,040
For each pair, the judge works from the
rubric saved with the trials, and gives a

51
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:09,725
verdict from the treatment's side.

52
00:03:10,225 --> 00:03:14,625
Much better scores plus one, and slightly
better, plus zero point four.

53
00:03:15,125 --> 00:03:16,850
Ties score zero.

54
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:20,400
The worse verdicts mirror those, below
zero.

55
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:24,325
Each pair costs two judge calls, one for
each order.

56
00:03:25,533 --> 00:03:29,658
Here's what compare printed in an earlier
run, on other saved trials.

57
00:03:30,158 --> 00:03:34,758
First, the win rate is the share of pairs
that the treatment won: six of nine here.

58
00:03:35,258 --> 00:03:39,233
Ties count against it, so a win rate isn't
the same as not worse.

59
00:03:39,733 --> 00:03:43,483
Next is the mean of the nine scores, with
a range around it.

60
00:03:43,983 --> 00:03:48,324
That range is a confidence interval: the
kind we put around a count, here around a

61
00:03:48,324 --> 00:03:50,758
mean, and the true mean is probably inside
it.

62
00:03:51,258 --> 00:03:53,883
This one lies wholly above zero.

63
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:58,750
Below that is a second test, and it
ignores the judge.

64
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:03,327
This test looks only at the graders' pass
or fail, and counts the pairs that

65
00:04:03,327 --> 00:04:06,600
flipped: five went from fail to pass, and
none the other way.

66
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:11,418
You can see the test's name on that line,
and it asks whether a split like five to

67
00:04:11,418 --> 00:04:13,050
nothing could easily be chance.

68
00:04:13,550 --> 00:04:18,500
From chance alone, a split that lopsided
turns up about six times in a hundred.

69
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,675
The usual bar for calling a result
significant is under five in a hundred.

70
00:04:24,175 --> 00:04:26,725
So this one is not significant.

71
00:04:27,225 --> 00:04:31,064
Last come the metric changes: the
treatment minus the baseline for each

72
00:04:31,064 --> 00:04:33,875
measure, averaged over the pairs, each
with a range.

73
00:04:34,375 --> 00:04:38,400
The words high variance mean the pairs
differed a lot from one another.

74
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:44,525
One option turns compare into a regression
check: dash dash fail on regression.

75
00:04:45,025 --> 00:04:49,475
With it, the command exits one when the
treatment is significantly worse.

76
00:04:49,975 --> 00:04:54,800
That means its mean score is below zero,
and its whole range is below zero too.

77
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:59,400
So a negative mean isn't enough if the
range reaches above zero.

78
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:04,443
Nothing else counts: a rise in tokens
doesn't change the exit code, and neither

79
00:05:04,443 --> 00:05:05,650
does the second test.

80
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:10,398
Apart from that, with or without the
option, compare exits one when some pair

81
00:05:10,398 --> 00:05:12,550
couldn't be judged, even after retries.

82
00:05:13,050 --> 00:05:17,477
To print the exit code, there's echo
dollar question mark, typed straight after

83
00:05:17,477 --> 00:05:18,150
the command.

84
00:05:19,367 --> 00:05:23,226
That's how compare works; now let's run it
on the saved trials of our

85
00:05:23,226 --> 00:05:24,792
before-and-after experiment.

86
00:05:25,292 --> 00:05:29,017
First, the before skill against the after
skill, on haiku.

87
00:05:29,517 --> 00:05:32,867
Then the same on terra, with the
regression check on.

88
00:05:33,367 --> 00:05:37,744
Last, haiku again with the two roles
swapped, so the before skill becomes the

89
00:05:37,744 --> 00:05:38,767
change under test.

90
00:05:39,267 --> 00:05:42,417
Which of the three would you expect the
check to fail?

91
00:05:44,917 --> 00:05:47,792
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

92
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,250
All three run from inside the experiment's
folder, so we change into it.

93
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:57,500
Let's see what it holds.

94
00:05:58,327 --> 00:06:02,477
There's one folder for each variant,
beside the experiment's report.

95
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:11,039
First, haiku: the before skill as the
baseline, and the after skill as the

96
00:06:11,039 --> 00:06:11,625
treatment.

97
00:06:12,125 --> 00:06:15,950
It spends eighteen judge calls, two for
each of nine pairs.

98
00:06:16,450 --> 00:06:20,125
We're after the mean score, and whether
its range clears zero.

99
00:06:23,587 --> 00:06:28,134
About two minutes later, the treatment has
won four of nine pairs, and the mean is

100
00:06:28,134 --> 00:06:29,687
plus zero point three three.

101
00:06:34,187 --> 00:06:39,330
Its range runs from minus zero point zero
eight to plus zero point seven five, so it

102
00:06:39,330 --> 00:06:40,187
includes zero.

103
00:06:43,687 --> 00:06:48,306
On tokens, the range is wholly below zero:
the after skill used about fourteen

104
00:06:48,306 --> 00:06:49,787
thousand fewer per trial.

105
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,581
The same command also wrote its record:
one line of JSON, spread out here so we

106
00:06:58,581 --> 00:06:59,625
can go through it.

107
00:07:00,125 --> 00:07:04,000
This pair is the dates request, trial zero
from each version.

108
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:09,276
The pairwise judge found the after skill's
entry slightly worse, for a score of minus

109
00:07:09,276 --> 00:07:10,175
zero point four.

110
00:07:10,675 --> 00:07:15,166
Its reason is kept too: the before entry
adds an example date, which makes the

111
00:07:15,166 --> 00:07:16,375
change more concrete.

112
00:07:19,875 --> 00:07:23,300
On this same pair, both entries had passed
their graders.

113
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:28,050
In another pair, the two orders agreed on
the winner but not on how much, so the

114
00:07:28,050 --> 00:07:29,750
record keeps the weaker verdict.

115
00:07:32,967 --> 00:07:37,567
Now the second model, with the regression
check on, and a record file of its own.

116
00:07:38,067 --> 00:07:42,321
On the result, there are two things to
look at: the mean score's range, and the

117
00:07:42,321 --> 00:07:42,967
tokens line.

118
00:07:51,287 --> 00:07:55,587
This time only six pairs were judged:
three of the before trials did not finish,

119
00:07:55,587 --> 00:07:58,437
so their partners were left out, as the
warning says.

120
00:08:02,937 --> 00:08:07,548
The judged score barely moved: three of
the six are ties, and the range includes

121
00:08:07,548 --> 00:08:07,837
zero.

122
00:08:11,337 --> 00:08:16,187
The tokens fell, by about eight thousand
per trial, with a range wholly below zero.

123
00:08:19,687 --> 00:08:21,562
So did the check fail?

124
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:26,140
Exit code zero: no regression in the
judged score.

125
00:08:29,367 --> 00:08:33,470
To see whether the check fails, we swap
the two roles: the after skill is the

126
00:08:33,470 --> 00:08:36,667
baseline now, and the before skill is the
change under test.

127
00:08:37,167 --> 00:08:41,640
A consistent judge would print the first
comparison's mean with its sign flipped:

128
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:43,242
minus zero point three three.

129
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:52,365
This one printed exactly that, and its
range reaches above zero.

130
00:08:56,865 --> 00:08:59,315
So what does the check make of that?

131
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:05,140
Exit code zero: the check passed, because
the range isn't wholly below zero.

132
00:09:08,367 --> 00:09:10,892
Here are all three, side by side.

133
00:09:11,392 --> 00:09:14,117
So which of the three did the check fail?

134
00:09:14,617 --> 00:09:16,592
None of them, in our run.

135
00:09:17,092 --> 00:09:22,132
The swapped comparison came closest: its
mean is below zero, but its range crosses

136
00:09:22,132 --> 00:09:22,317
it.

137
00:09:25,533 --> 00:09:27,508
So what does this support?

138
00:09:28,008 --> 00:09:32,550
Counting passes on haiku gave two ranges
that overlapped, so the counts couldn't

139
00:09:32,550 --> 00:09:32,833
tell.

140
00:09:33,333 --> 00:09:37,798
The second test says the same: four pairs
flipped from fail to pass, none flipped

141
00:09:37,798 --> 00:09:40,333
back, and it still calls that not
significant.

142
00:09:40,833 --> 00:09:45,744
Its number is coin-toss odds: four tosses
all landing the same way happen one time

143
00:09:45,744 --> 00:09:46,283
in eight.

144
00:09:46,783 --> 00:09:51,230
The pairwise judge leans the same way,
with a mean above zero, but its range

145
00:09:51,230 --> 00:09:52,283
includes zero too.

146
00:09:52,783 --> 00:09:57,493
So in our run, all three agree: the edit
looks better, and these trials can't yet

147
00:09:57,493 --> 00:09:57,958
show it.

148
00:09:58,458 --> 00:10:02,735
An earlier run, on other saved trials,
went further: there the pairwise range was

149
00:10:02,735 --> 00:10:05,533
wholly above zero, while the counts still
overlapped.

150
00:10:06,033 --> 00:10:10,271
A pairwise judge can do that, because it
compares the two entries of each pair

151
00:10:10,271 --> 00:10:13,258
directly, and sees differences that pass
or fail can't.

152
00:10:14,467 --> 00:10:17,142
Hold that loosely, for three reasons.

153
00:10:17,642 --> 00:10:21,450
First, the nine pairs aren't nine
independent requests: they're three

154
00:10:21,450 --> 00:10:23,217
stimuli, tried three times each.

155
00:10:23,717 --> 00:10:27,367
So the range looks narrower than the
evidence really allows.

156
00:10:27,867 --> 00:10:32,128
Next, the judge is a model, and this
pairwise judge hasn't been checked against

157
00:10:32,128 --> 00:10:34,717
our own labels, the way the yes-or-no
judge was.

158
00:10:35,217 --> 00:10:38,142
Third, it varies from one run to the next.

159
00:10:38,642 --> 00:10:42,604
Two earlier runs of the swapped
comparison, both on the same saved trials,

160
00:10:42,604 --> 00:10:46,567
landed on both sides of the line: one
failed the check, and one passed it.

161
00:10:47,067 --> 00:10:51,217
Our own run passed it too, with a range
that reaches above zero.

162
00:10:51,717 --> 00:10:56,267
So trust the sign, and whether the range
crosses zero, not the second decimal.

163
00:10:57,467 --> 00:11:00,717
Now the other half of the question: worse
in cost.

164
00:11:01,217 --> 00:11:05,667
In our run, the edit made nothing cost
more: tokens fell on both models.

165
00:11:06,167 --> 00:11:10,824
In an earlier run, on other saved trials,
the after skill took about eleven thousand

166
00:11:10,824 --> 00:11:15,094
more tokens per trial on terra, with a
range wholly above zero, and the check

167
00:11:15,094 --> 00:11:16,092
still exited zero.

168
00:11:16,592 --> 00:11:20,192
That's by design: the check looks at the
judged score alone.

169
00:11:20,692 --> 00:11:25,120
So cost needs a guard of its own: the
budget graders, with their limits set from

170
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:26,117
the before trials.

171
00:11:26,617 --> 00:11:31,167
Then an edit that costs more fails a
trial, like any other grader's failure.

172
00:11:32,367 --> 00:11:36,524
One last piece of how this works: an
experiment can run the same judging as

173
00:11:36,524 --> 00:11:38,742
soon as it ends, with dash dash compare.

174
00:11:39,242 --> 00:11:43,742
That option only reports: a regression it
finds doesn't fail the command.

175
00:11:44,242 --> 00:11:49,007
Vally's own guide gives the order: run the
experiment, then run compare on its folder

176
00:11:49,007 --> 00:11:51,867
with fail on regression, as the step that
can fail.

177
00:11:53,067 --> 00:11:57,417
Before you go on: what has to be true for
the regression check to exit one?

178
00:12:00,417 --> 00:12:03,617
First, the treatment's mean score is below
zero.

179
00:12:04,117 --> 00:12:07,217
Second, its whole range is below zero too.

180
00:12:07,717 --> 00:12:10,342
Nothing else that compare prints counts.

181
00:12:11,567 --> 00:12:16,212
You can now judge two saved runs side by
side, and run a check that fails on a

182
00:12:16,212 --> 00:12:16,867
regression.

183
00:12:17,367 --> 00:12:21,623
At this point there are many moving parts,
and runs sometimes end in error, or fail

184
00:12:21,623 --> 00:12:23,367
for reasons that aren't the skill.

185
00:12:23,867 --> 00:12:26,667
When a run goes wrong, where do you look
first?

186
00:12:27,167 --> 00:12:29,017
That's the next lesson.

187
00:12:29,517 --> 00:12:33,092
The exercises on the lesson page are worth
doing first.
